{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"mvp2-notation","type":"text","text":"<span class=\"wan-scene-style wan-scene-math wan-scene-grid\"></span>\n\nConsider downscaling the frame of the trained model. We compare the clip of a configuration with the clip of the $704\\times1280$ reference with the same seed.\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\mathrm{cfg}}_{\\text{configuration}},\\quad\n\\underbrace{\\mathrm{cfg}_0}_{\\text{reference }704\\times1280},\\quad\n\\underbrace{s}_{\\text{seed}},\\quad\n\\underbrace{\\mathrm{video}(\\mathrm{cfg},s)}_{\\text{clip}}.\n$$\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\mathcal L=\\mathrm{LPIPS}_{\\rm align}+\\bigl(1-\\mathrm{DINO}\\bigr)}_{\\text{loss of the clip to the reference with the same seed}},\\quad\n\\underbrace{\\mathcal L_1}_{\\text{loss of the first chunk}},\\quad\n\\underbrace{\\overline{\\mathcal L}}_{\\text{mean over 3 seeds and 4 scenes}}.\n$$\n$$\n\\underbrace{H\\times W}_{\\text{frame}},\\quad\n\\underbrace{h\\times w=\\tfrac{H}{32}\\times\\tfrac{W}{32}}_{\\text{token grid of the frame}},\\quad\n\\underbrace{F_{\\rm chunk},F_{\\rm anch},F_{\\rm win}}_{\\text{latent frames: chunk, anchor, window}},\\quad\n\\underbrace{T_{\\rm block}}_{\\text{time of one DiT block}}.\n$$\n\nNow consider what we measure the loss with and which configuration we descend from.\n","x":0,"y":-180,"width":1780,"height":478},
		{"id":"mvp2-loss","type":"text","text":"<span class=\"wan-scene-style wan-scene-math wan-scene-grid\"></span>\n# Loss measurement · MVP2\n\nHow well does the loss to the reference distinguish configurations? Consider two clips and the distance between them.\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\mathcal L(a,b)}_{\\text{loss of clip }a\\text{ to clip }b},\\qquad\n\\underbrace{\\mathcal L_{\\rm floor}=\\mathcal L\\bigl(\\mathrm{video}'(\\mathrm{cfg}_0,s),\\mathrm{video}(\\mathrm{cfg}_0,s)\\bigr)}_{\\text{repeat of the same configuration}},\\qquad\n\\underbrace{\\mathcal L_{\\rm ceil}=\\mathcal L\\bigl(\\mathrm{video}(\\mathrm{cfg}_0,s'),\\mathrm{video}(\\mathrm{cfg}_0,s)\\bigr)}_{\\text{another seed}}\n$$\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\mathcal L-\\mathcal L_{\\rm floor}}{\\mathcal L_{\\rm ceil}-\\mathcal L_{\\rm floor}}}_{\\text{share of the gap between the repeat and another clip}}\n$$\n\n| Transformation                                                                                                                                          | Context → computation                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          | Idea                                                                                                                  | Observations                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| :------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | :----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | :-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | :-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| $\\displaystyle \\mathrm{video}_0\\mapsto\\operatorname{resize}_{608}(\\mathrm{video}_0)$                                                                    | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{Downscale the finished reference by area, without generation.}\\\\[6pt]&\\mathcal L\\bigl(\\operatorname{resize}_{608}(\\mathrm{video}_0),\\mathrm{video}_0\\bigr)\\end{aligned}$                                                                                                                            | The price of the pixel loss itself.                                                                                           | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{0.013}_{\\text{fox}},\\quad\\underbrace{0.008}_{\\text{cup}}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{3 chunks, seed 0}\\end{aligned}$                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| $\\displaystyle \\mathrm{cfg}_0\\mapsto\\mathrm{cfg}_0$<br>Repeat without changes.                                                                            | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{Consider float summation from parallel kernel tiles.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\Sigma_{\\rm atomic}}_{\\text{order of summands is not fixed}}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\overline K}_{\\text{mean of the keys before Sage}},\\qquad\\underbrace{x\\,W_{\\rm down}}_{\\text{low-rank branch of W4A4}}\\end{aligned}$ | We check whether one config gives one clip. Two sources: our key preparation kernel and the Nunchaku kernel.                | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{36\\text{–}38\\%}_{\\text{divergence of the first chunk latent}}\\\\[6pt]&\\mathcal L_{\\rm floor}:\\quad\\underbrace{0.26}_{\\text{fox}},\\quad\\underbrace{0.18}_{\\text{cup}}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{The divergence starts at block 0.}\\end{aligned}$<br>[Report, section 3](docs/report-2026-11-11-loss-704-608-for-fable.md) |\n| <span class=\"wan-kept\">Applied</span><br>$\\displaystyle \\Sigma_{\\rm atomic}\\mapsto\\Sigma_{\\rm ordered}$<br>`kernels.lora_sum`                         | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{Each tile writes its own partial sum.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{p_1,\\ldots,p_m}_{\\text{partial sums of the tiles}}\\\\[6pt]&\\Sigma_{\\rm ordered}=\\bigl((p_1+p_2)+\\cdots\\bigr)+p_m\\\\[6pt]&\\text{We sum in a fixed order.}\\end{aligned}$                                                | One config gives one clip: the repeat is comparable bit by bit. In experiments ordered, in Live atomic.                          | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\mathcal L_{\\rm floor}=0\\\\[6pt]&\\text{The latent and MP4 of two repeats are bit-for-bit equal.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{T_{\\rm block}:38.0\\to38.8\\ \\mathrm{ms}}_{608\\times1120},\\quad\\mathrm{FPS}:-2\\%\\end{aligned}$<br>[Report, section 11](docs/report-2026-11-11-loss-704-608-for-fable.md)                                             |\n| $\\displaystyle M:1\\mapsto1+10^{-5}$<br>`rope.m`                                                                                                         | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{One small perturbation, three variants of weights and attention.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\delta=\\frac{\\lVert\\mathrm{lat}'-\\mathrm{lat}\\rVert}{\\lVert\\mathrm{lat}\\rVert}}_{\\text{divergence of the first chunk latent}}\\\\[6pt]&704\\times1280,\\quad\\text{fox},\\quad\\Sigma_{\\rm ordered}\\end{aligned}$       | Where the amplification of a small error comes from: the two-step model or the 4 bits.                                                     | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{\\delta=0.35}_{\\mathrm{W4A4}+\\mathrm{Sage\\,2}}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\delta=0.18}_{\\mathrm{FP8}+\\mathrm{Sage\\,2}}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\delta=0.16}_{\\mathrm{FP8}+\\mathrm{SDPA}}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{The model itself amplifies; W4A4 doubles it.}\\end{aligned}$                                                            |\n| <span class=\"wan-kept\">Applied</span><br>$\\displaystyle \\mathcal L\\mapsto\\overline{\\mathcal L}_1$                                                     | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{We average the loss of the first chunk.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{s\\in\\{0,1,2\\}}_{\\text{seed}},\\quad\\underbrace{4\\ \\text{scenes}}_{\\text{cup, fox, astronaut, two foxes}}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{Order of the chains as in the reference:}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{the seed noise runs consecutively through the chains.}\\end{aligned}$                 | The loss accumulates already in the first chunk; one point costs about 5 s. Averaging removes the dependence on a single trajectory. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{\\overline{\\mathcal L}_1=0.360,\\quad\\overline{\\mathcal L}=0.398}_{608\\times1120}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\mathcal L_{\\rm ceil}=0.736}_{\\text{first chunk}},\\quad\\underbrace{0.753}_{\\text{clip}}\\end{aligned}$<br>[Report, section 27](docs/report-2026-11-11-loss-704-608-for-fable.md)                        |\n| <span class=\"wan-kept\">Applied</span><br>$\\displaystyle \\epsilon\\mapsto\\operatorname{spectral}_{h\\times w}(\\epsilon_{22\\times40})$<br>`descent.noise` | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{We get the noise of the downscaled frame from the noise of the reference.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\epsilon_{22\\times40}}_{\\text{reference noise}}\\xrightarrow{\\text{truncate the spectrum}}\\underbrace{\\epsilon_{h\\times w}}_{\\text{noise of the downscaled frame}}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{Computed on the CPU at every chunk.}\\end{aligned}$                 | The reference and the downscaled frame share the low frequencies of the noise. For comparison only; in Live the noise is fresh.                     | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{0.43\\ \\mathrm{s}}_{\\text{per chunk}}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\mathrm{FPS}:8.60\\to9.73}_{608\\times1120,\\ \\text{fresh noise}}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{We measure FPS with fresh noise,}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{the loss with the spectral one.}\\end{aligned}$                                                                                        |\n\n[Report 11–12.11](docs/report-2026-11-11-loss-704-608-for-fable.md) · [Measurement plan](docs/handoff-2026-11-11-loss-search.md)\n","x":0,"y":340,"width":1780,"height":1730},
		{"id":"mvp2-start","type":"text","text":"<span class=\"wan-scene-style wan-scene-math wan-scene-grid\"></span>\n# Starting point · MVP2\n\nWhich configuration should the descent start from? Consider a configuration as a set of factors; a step changes one factor.\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\mathrm{cfg}=(W_{\\rm DiT},W_{\\rm T5},\\mathrm{KV},\\mathrm{Attn},\\mathrm{RoPE},H\\times W)}_{\\text{factors}},\\qquad\n\\underbrace{\\mathrm{cfg}^{(k)}\\mapsto\\mathrm{cfg}^{(k+1)}}_{\\text{change one factor}},\\qquad\n\\underbrace{704\\times1280,\\quad\\mathrm{KV}=\\mathrm{FP8},\\quad W_{\\rm T5}=\\mathrm{BF16}}_{\\text{common to all steps}}\n$$\n\n| Transformation | Context → computation | Idea | Observations |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| <span class=\"wan-kept\">Applied</span><br>$\\displaystyle \\mathrm{Attn}_{\\rm self}:\\ \\mathrm{SDPA}\\mapsto\\mathrm{Sage\\,2}$ | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{Consider vertex A′: FP8, SDPA.}\\\\[6pt]&\\mathrm{Attn}_{\\rm self}=\\mathrm{Sage\\,2},\\quad\\mathrm{RoPE}=\\text{separate LongLive kernel}\\end{aligned}$ | We change only the self-attention kernel. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\mathrm{FPS}:1.30\\to2.17\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{24\\text{–}27\\ \\mathrm{dB}}_{\\text{PSNR of the first chunk to A′}}\\end{aligned}$<br>[E29](notes/research/cards/E29.%20Вершина%20A′%20и%20спуск%20B,%20FP8,%20704×1280.md) |\n| <span class=\"wan-kept\">Applied</span><br>$\\displaystyle W_{\\rm DiT}:\\ \\mathrm{FP8}\\mapsto\\mathrm{W4A4}$ | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{Weights and activations of the linear layers in 4 bits.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\mathrm{LongLive\\,2.0\\ S2}}_{\\text{trained and distilled in NVFP4}}\\end{aligned}$ | For this checkpoint, closer to FP32 does not mean better video. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\mathrm{FPS}:2.17\\to5.41\\\\[6pt]&\\text{By the grid: not worse than FP8.}\\end{aligned}$<br>[E30](notes/research/cards/E30.%20Стартовая%20точка%20W4A4%20704×1280%20и%20отложенные%20шаги.md) |\n| $\\displaystyle W_{\\rm T5}:\\ \\mathrm{BF16}\\mapsto\\mathrm{INT8}$ | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{We quantize the text encoder.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{T_{\\rm prompt}}_{\\text{prompt change time}}\\end{aligned}$ | Faster prompt change. Not applied: postponed to the end. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\mathrm{FPS}:5.41\\to5.58\\\\[6pt]&T_{\\rm prompt}:2.86\\to1.63\\ \\mathrm{s}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{User: the prompt is matched worse.}\\end{aligned}$<br>[E30](notes/research/cards/E30.%20Стартовая%20точка%20W4A4%20704×1280%20и%20отложенные%20шаги.md) |\n| $\\displaystyle \\mathrm{RoPE}\\overset{\\rm fuse}{\\hookrightarrow}\\mathrm{Attn}_{\\rm Sage}$ | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{Fused kernel with LongLive rounding.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{0.029\\%}_{\\text{difference from the start per call}}\\end{aligned}$ | We remove the separate RoPE pass. Not applied: gives no speed. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\mathrm{FPS}:5.41\\to5.45\\\\[6pt]&\\text{The video diverges like another seed.}\\end{aligned}$<br>[E30](notes/research/cards/E30.%20Стартовая%20точка%20W4A4%20704×1280%20и%20отложенные%20шаги.md) |\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\mathrm{cfg}_{\\rm start}=\\mathrm{point\\mbox{-}b2}}_{\\text{W4A4, Sage 2, T5 BF16, KV FP8, }704\\times1280},\\qquad\\mathrm{FPS}=5.41.\n$$\n\n[Starting point config](configs/experiments/director-original/point-b2-w4a4-sage-704x1280.json)\n","x":1900,"y":-180,"width":1700,"height":1018},
		{"id":"mvp2-rope","type":"text","text":"<span class=\"wan-scene-style wan-scene-math wan-scene-grid\"></span>\n# Resolution change and RoPE · MVP2\n\nHow to reduce the token grid with the smallest loss? Consider the RoPE frequencies of one spatial axis.\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(h,w):(22,40)\\mapsto(h',w')}_{\\text{token grid of the frame}},\\qquad\n\\underbrace{\\theta_j=10000^{-2j/42},\\quad j=0,\\ldots,20}_{\\text{RoPE frequencies of the axis}},\\qquad\n\\underbrace{M_H,M_W}_{\\text{multipliers for height and width}}\n$$\n$$\n\\theta'_{a,j}=\n\\begin{cases}\nM_a\\,\\theta_j,&\\mathrm{PI},\\\\\nM_a^{\\,j/20}\\,\\theta_j,&\\mathrm{NTK},\\\\\nM_a^{\\,\\mu+(1-\\mu)j/20}\\,\\theta_j,&\\mathrm{mix}(\\mu),\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad a\\in\\{H,W\\}.\n$$\n\nWe do not change the time axis. Overview of papers — [[Позиции RoPE|RoPE positions]].\n\n| Transformation | Context → computation | Idea | Observations |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| $\\displaystyle \\theta_j\\mapsto M^{\\,j/20}\\theta_j$<br>NTK<br>[YaRN, App. A.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2309.00071) | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{We keep the high frequencies and stretch the low ones.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{M^\\star(h')=\\arg\\min_M\\mathcal L}_{\\text{best }M\\text{ at the step}}\\end{aligned}$ | We check whether the best $M$ grows as the grid shrinks. Not applied. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{M^\\star\\approx1.2\\text{–}1.3}_{672,\\ 640,\\ 608}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{Almost constant, does not grow.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\overline{\\mathcal L}_1=0.436}_{\\text{held-out scenes, }M=1.2}\\end{aligned}$<br>[Descent 09.11](D:/Experiments/DIT/outputs/res-descent-2026-11-09/REPORT.md) |\n| $\\displaystyle \\mathrm{shift}\\mapsto\\mathrm{scaleArea}\\cdot\\mathrm{shift}$<br>[Scaling Rectified Flow, (23)](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.03206#S5.SS3.SSS2) | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{We change the noise schedule for a smaller frame.}\\\\[6pt]&576\\times1056:\\quad 5\\mapsto4.11\\mapsto3.63\\end{aligned}$ | Fewer tokens, smaller shift. Not applied. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{There is no stable improvement.}\\\\[6pt]&\\mathrm{shift}=5\\end{aligned}$ |\n| $\\displaystyle (M_H,M_W,\\mu)\\mapsto\\arg\\min\\overline{\\mathcal L}_1$<br>CMA-ES | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{We search for the profile on the training scenes and check on the held-out ones.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\text{cup, fox}}_{\\text{training}},\\quad\\underbrace{\\text{astronaut, two foxes}}_{\\text{held-out}}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{144\\ \\text{points}}_{\\text{seeds 0 and 1}},\\quad\\underbrace{\\text{seed 2}}_{\\text{held-out}}\\end{aligned}$ | Is there a profile better than one number per axis. Not applied: fitting to the training scenes. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&(M_H,M_W,\\mu)=(1.134,\\ 1.104,\\ 1)\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{0.338}_{\\text{training}},\\quad\\underbrace{0.382}_{\\text{held-out}}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{Plateau from the third generation.}\\end{aligned}$<br>[Report, section 14](docs/report-2026-11-11-loss-704-608-for-fable.md) |\n| <span class=\"wan-kept\">Applied</span><br>$\\displaystyle M_H=\\frac{22}{h'},\\qquad M_W=\\frac{40}{w'}$<br>PI by the grid ratio<br>[Position Interpolation, §2.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2306.15595) | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{We stretch the positions of the downscaled frame to the trained grid.}\\\\[6pt]&\\mathrm{pos}\\mapsto M_a\\,\\mathrm{pos},\\qquad\\underbrace{M_a(h'-1)\\approx h-1}_{\\text{the same range of positions}}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{608\\times1120:\\ M_H=\\tfrac{22}{19},\\ M_W=\\tfrac{40}{35}}_{\\text{check on held-out scenes}}\\end{aligned}$ | No tuning: the multiplier is set by the grids. The object keeps its position and scale in the frame. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{0.387}_{\\text{held-out, }608}\\ \\text{versus}\\ \\underbrace{0.382}_{\\rm CMA}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{0.442}_{\\text{PI}},\\ \\underbrace{0.619}_{\\text{no interpolation}},\\ \\underbrace{0.607}_{\\text{uniform }1.1}\\quad(512,\\ \\text{seed }0)\\\\[6pt]&\\text{User: the objects stand where they are in the reference.}\\end{aligned}$ |\n| $\\displaystyle 17\\times30\\mapsto17\\times31$<br>Uniform stretching | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{544\\times960:\\ M_H=1.294,\\ M_W=1.333}_{\\text{non-uniform}}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{544\\times992:\\ M_H=1.294,\\ M_W=1.290}_{\\text{as in the reference}}\\end{aligned}$ | We check whether different stretching of the axes spoils the loss. Not applied. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\overline{\\mathcal L}_1:\\quad\\underbrace{0.426}_{544\\times960},\\quad\\underbrace{0.436}_{544\\times992}\\\\[6pt]&\\mathrm{FPS}:-6\\%\\end{aligned}$<br>[Report, section 30](docs/report-2026-11-11-loss-704-608-for-fable.md) |\n\n**Resolution ladder**\n\nPI by the grid ratio, $\\Sigma_{\\rm ordered}$, FPS of the last chunk of the fox with fresh noise, before Sage 2 in cross-attention.\n\n| $H\\times W$ | $h\\times w$ | FPS, $F_{\\rm win}=32$ | FPS, $F_{\\rm win}=24$ | $\\overline{\\mathcal L}_1$ | $\\overline{\\mathcal L}_1/\\mathcal L_{\\rm ceil}$ |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| $704\\times1280$ | $22\\times40$ | $5.71$ | $6.58$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $608\\times1120$ | $19\\times35$ | $8.51$ | $9.61$ | $0.360$ | $0.49$ |\n| $576\\times1056$ | $18\\times33$ | $9.90$ | $11.14$ | $0.393$ | $0.53$ |\n| $544\\times992$ | $17\\times31$ | $11.83$ | $13.20$ | $0.414$ | $0.56$ |\n| $544\\times960$ | $17\\times30$ | $12.53$ | $13.94$ | $0.426$ | $0.58$ |\n| $512\\times928$ | $16\\times29$ | $14.18$ | $15.73$ | $0.483$ | $0.66$ |\n\nThe step $544\\mapsto512$ costs $0.069$ of loss; the previous steps cost $0.02$–$0.03$.\n\n[Grids](D:/Experiments/DIT/outputs/speed-2026-11-12/grids/) · [NTK hypothesis](notes/research/NTK-zoom-hypothesis.md) · [Frequency-aware](notes/research/Frequency-aware-RoPE.md)\n","x":3720,"y":-180,"width":1800,"height":2060},
		{"id":"mvp2-memory","type":"text","text":"<span class=\"wan-scene-style wan-scene-math wan-scene-grid\"></span>\n# Video memory · MVP2\n\nCan the video memory be made cheaper without losing the scene? Consider the generation of one chunk: two denoising steps and a write pass.\n\n$$\n(\\epsilon_{n+1},\\mathrm{ctx},\\mathrm{KVvid}_n)\n\\xrightarrow{\\ t_1\\ }\\widehat{\\mathrm{lat}}_{n+1}\n\\xrightarrow{\\ t_2=833\\ }\\mathrm{lat}_{n+1}\n\\xrightarrow{\\ t=0,\\ \\text{write}\\ }\\mathrm{KVvid}_{n+1}\n$$\n$$\n\\underbrace{T_{\\rm chunk}\\approx3\\cdot30\\cdot T_{\\rm block}}_{\\text{three passes over 30 blocks}},\\qquad\n\\underbrace{\\mathrm{KVvid}_n=\\mathrm{KVanch}\\oplus\\mathrm{KVupd}_n}_{\\text{anchor and ring}},\\qquad\n\\underbrace{(F_{\\rm chunk},F_{\\rm anch},F_{\\rm win})=(8,8,32)}_{\\text{MVP1}}\n$$\n\nMVP1 memory rules — [[Длительная генерация|Long generation]].\n\n| Transformation | Context → computation | Idea | Observations |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| $\\displaystyle F_{\\rm win}:32\\mapsto24$ | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{The ring is shorter by one chunk.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{F_{\\rm win}-F_{\\rm anch}-F_{\\rm chunk}:\\ 16\\mapsto8}_{\\text{frames in the ring}}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{The window takes effect when the ring is full.}\\end{aligned}$ | Fewer keys in self-attention. Not applied: risk of drift and a failure at a scene change. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\mathrm{FPS}:+12\\text{–}15\\%\\\\[6pt]&\\text{The first three chunks are bit-for-bit equal.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\lvert\\Delta B\\rvert>15}_{\\text{blue shift of the fox, 12 chunks}}:\\ \\underbrace{3\\ \\text{of}\\ 12}_{F_{\\rm win}=24},\\quad\\underbrace{0\\ \\text{of}\\ 12}_{F_{\\rm win}=32}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{Fails at a scene change: the ring equals one chunk.}\\end{aligned}$<br>[Report, section 27](docs/report-2026-11-11-loss-704-608-for-fable.md) |\n| $\\displaystyle F_{\\rm win}:32\\mapsto16$ | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{No ring: anchor and new chunk.}\\\\[6pt]&16=8+0+8\\end{aligned}$ | The limit of shrinking the window. Not applied. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{\\mathrm{FPS}:+27\\%}_{608\\times1120}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{3.86\\ /\\ 4.68}_{\\text{seam: cup / fox}}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{The cup empties, the frame resets.}\\end{aligned}$ |\n| $\\displaystyle \\mathrm{KVnew}_{n+1}:\\ t=0\\mapsto t_2$<br>`cache_write: last_step` | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{We take K/V from the last denoising step.}\\\\[6pt]&T_{\\rm chunk}:\\ 3\\cdot30\\,T_{\\rm block}\\mapsto2\\cdot30\\,T_{\\rm block}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\cos\\bigl(V^{t_2},V^{t=0}\\bigr)}_{\\text{similarity to the clean write, per block}}\\end{aligned}$ | We remove a third of the chunk. Not applied: the video memory breaks. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{\\cos V=0.43,\\quad\\cos K=0.76}_{t_2=833}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\mathrm{FPS}:8.60\\to11.25}_{608\\times1120}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{Water in the cup does not fill;}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{the fox's camera drifts away.}\\end{aligned}$<br>[Report, part 3](docs/report-2026-11-11-loss-704-608-for-fable.md) |\n| $\\displaystyle t_2:833\\mapsto t$<br>Write at low noise | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{We add noise to the clean chunk latent up to }t\text{ and compare K/V.}\\\\[6pt]&t\\in\\{600,400,250,100\\}\\end{aligned}$ | Is there a step time at which K/V are close to the clean ones. Not applied. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\cos V:\\quad0.48,\\ 0.58,\\ 0.66,\\ 0.70\\\\[6pt]&\\text{K/V depend on the modulation by }t\text{.}\\end{aligned}$ |\n| $\\displaystyle \\mathrm{KVvid}_n\\mapsto\\mathrm{KVanch}$<br>in the write pass<br>`clean_pass_sink` | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{In the write pass, attention only on the anchor and the chunk.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{T_{\\rm Sage}:12.0\\to4.1\\ \\mathrm{ms}}_{\\text{estimate from the first chunk}}\\end{aligned}$ | We make the third pass cheaper. Not applied. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\mathrm{FPS}:-1.4\\%\\\\[6pt]&\\text{The mask adds its own overhead.}\\end{aligned}$ |\n| <span class=\"wan-kept\">Applied</span><br>$\\displaystyle \\Phi_{\\rm continue}^{(2)}=\\Phi_{\\rm continue}^{(1)}$ | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&(F_{\\rm chunk},F_{\\rm anch},F_{\\rm win})=(8,8,32)\\\\[6pt]&\\text{A separate write pass at }t=0\text{.}\\end{aligned}$ | We keep the MVP1 video memory unchanged. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{0\\ \\text{of}\\ 12}_{\\text{strong color drift}}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{1.71\\ /\\ 1.13}_{\\text{seam: fox / cup, }512}\\end{aligned}$ |\n\n[Window grids](D:/Experiments/DIT/outputs/kv-window-2026-11-11/) · [Report 11–12.11](docs/report-2026-11-11-loss-704-608-for-fable.md)\n","x":5640,"y":-180,"width":1800,"height":1508},
		{"id":"mvp2-speed","type":"text","text":"<span class=\"wan-scene-style wan-scene-math wan-scene-grid\"></span>\n# Compilation and placement · MVP2\n\nWhat limits the chunk speed and what speeds up without changing the result? Consider the chunk time and the composition of one block.\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{T_{\\rm chunk}=3\\cdot30\\cdot T_{\\rm block}+T_{\\rm dec}}_{\\text{three passes and the decoder}},\\qquad\n\\underbrace{T_{\\rm block}=T_{\\rm GEMM}+T_{\\rm Sage}+T_{\\rm prep}+T_{\\rm norm}+\\cdots}_{\\text{kernels of one block}},\\qquad\n\\underbrace{\\mathrm{FPS}=\\frac{4\\,F_{\\rm chunk}}{T_{\\rm chunk}}}_{\\text{4 frames per latent frame}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{T_{\\rm block}=24.3\\ \\mathrm{ms}}_{512\\times928,\\ F_{\\rm win}=32}:\\qquad\n\\underbrace{38.6\\%}_{\\text{linear W4A4}},\\quad\n\\underbrace{37.9\\%}_{\\text{Sage self and cross}},\\quad\n\\underbrace{7.5\\%}_{\\text{Sage preparation}},\\quad\n\\underbrace{6.8\\%}_{\\text{norms, modulation}},\\quad\n\\underbrace{3.6\\%}_{\\text{activation quantization}}\n$$\n\nGraph and fusions of MVP1 — [Compilation · MVP1](MVP1%20EN.canvas). Placement — [[Offloading]].\n\n| Transformation | Context → computation | Idea | Observations |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| <span class=\"wan-kept\">Applied</span><br>$\\displaystyle \\mathrm{Attn}_{\\rm cross}:\\ \\mathrm{SDPA}\\mapsto\\mathrm{Sage\\,2}$ | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{Consider the cross-attention of the block.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\mathrm{KVtext}}_{\\text{computed once per prompt}}\\end{aligned}$ | The same kernel as in self-attention. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{\\mathrm{FPS}:14.18\\to14.71}_{512\\times928,\\ +3.7\\%}\\\\[6pt]&\\Delta\\overline{\\mathcal L}_1=+0.001\\end{aligned}$<br>[Report, part 3](docs/report-2026-11-11-loss-704-608-for-fable.md) |\n| <span class=\"wan-kept\">Applied</span><br>$\\displaystyle \\Sigma_{\\rm ordered}\\mapsto\\Sigma_{\\rm atomic}$<br>Live only | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{In Live a bit-exact repeat is not needed.}\\\\[6pt]&B_{\\rm VRAM}^{\\rm margin}:0.0625\\mapsto0\\end{aligned}$ | We return the fast summation order and give back the VRAM margin. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{\\mathrm{FPS}:14.71\\to14.82\\to15.05}_{512\\times928}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{13.08\\to13.44}_{544\\times960}\\end{aligned}$ |\n| $\\displaystyle \\mathrm{Attn}_{\\rm self}\\mapsto\\mathrm{Attn}_{\\rm sparse}$<br>`kernels.sparse` | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{We drop the keys with a small contribution.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\varepsilon=0.02,\\ \\text{anchor}+8}_{\\text{variant 1}},\\qquad\\underbrace{\\text{anchor}+\\text{last 4}}_{\\text{variant 2}}\\end{aligned}$ | Fewer keys in the Sage kernel. Not applied: with window 24 there are only three chunks of keys. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{+2.8\\%,\\quad\\Delta\\overline{\\mathcal L}=+0.023}_{\\text{variant 1}}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{+1.0\\%}_{\\text{variant 2}}\\end{aligned}$ |\n| $\\displaystyle T_{\\rm dec}\\parallel T_{\\rm block}$ | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{The decoder on its own stream on top of the next chunk.}\\\\[6pt]&T_{\\rm dec}=8\\cdot12.7\\ \\mathrm{ms}\\end{aligned}$ | We overlap the decoder with the blocks. Not applied: the GPU is already busy computing. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{The latent is bit-for-bit equal.}\\\\[6pt]&\\mathrm{FPS}:13.44\\to13.37\\\\[6pt]&M_{\\rm VRAM}=8.07\\ \\text{GB}\\end{aligned}$<br>[Report, section 30](docs/report-2026-11-11-loss-704-608-for-fable.md) |\n| $\\displaystyle \\mathrm{prep}(K_{\\rm win}):\\ 3\\mapsto1$<br>per chunk | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{The window of past chunks does not change between passes.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\overline K,\\ \\operatorname{scale}(V)}_{\\text{over all keys, including the new chunk}}\\end{aligned}$ | Do not recompute the preparation of the window keys. Not applied: impossible bit-for-bit. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{2.2\\ \\text{GB}}_{\\text{cache of the prepared window in VRAM}}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{\\le3.6\\%}_{\\text{estimate}}\\end{aligned}$ |\n| $\\displaystyle \\mathrm{Norm}\\to\\mathrm{Quant}_4\\mapsto\\mathrm{fused}$ | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{Norm, modulation and quantization in one kernel.}\\\\[6pt]&0.86+0.79\\ \\mathrm{ms}\\ \\text{per block}\\end{aligned}$ | We remove the write and the repeated read of BF16. Not done. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{\\approx2\\%}_{\\text{estimate}}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{A new kernel in the Nunchaku code.}\\end{aligned}$ |\n| <span class=\"wan-kept\">Applied</span><br>$\\displaystyle \\mathrm{policy}_2=\\mathrm{greedy\\_vram}$ | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\text{Consider the delivery of the weight rows.}\\\\[6pt]&\\underbrace{7.1\\ \\mathrm{ms}}_{\\text{row copy RAM → VRAM}}<\\underbrace{T_{\\rm block}}_{\\text{hidden behind the computation}}\\end{aligned}$ | We check whether the weight transfer is the limit. | $\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{1\\ \\mathrm{ms}}_{\\text{waiting for copies per run}},\\quad\\underbrace{0}_{\\text{late arrivals}}\\\\[6pt]&\\text{The speed is limited by the block computation.}\\end{aligned}$<br>[Report, section 12](docs/report-2026-11-11-loss-704-608-for-fable.md) |\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{T_{\\rm chunk}=2.39\\ \\mathrm{s}}_{544\\times960,\\ F_{\\rm win}=32}\n\\approx\\underbrace{90\\cdot25.2\\ \\mathrm{ms}}_{\\text{blocks}}+\\underbrace{0.10\\ \\mathrm{s}}_{\\text{decoder}},\\qquad\n\\mathrm{FPS}=\\frac{32}{2.39}\\approx13.4.\n$$\n\nTransformations with a bit-for-bit equal latent are exhausted. Further speed comes only from the linear W4A4 and Sage kernels.\n","x":7560,"y":-180,"width":1800,"height":1692},
		{"id":"mvp2-boundary","type":"text","text":"---\n\n**Choice of the working point 544×960, window 32 · next stage**","x":-80,"y":2620,"width":9520,"height":100},
		{"id":"mvp2-result","type":"text","text":"<span class=\"wan-scene-style wan-scene-math wan-scene-grid\"></span>\n# Working point · MVP2\n\nWhich configuration do we fix? Consider the candidates near the constraint $\\mathrm{FPS}\\ge15$.\n\n$$\n\\min_{\\mathrm{cfg}}\\ \\overline{\\mathcal L}_1(\\mathrm{cfg})\\quad\\text{subject to}\\quad\\mathrm{FPS}\\ge15,\\quad M_{\\rm VRAM}\\le8\\ \\text{GB},\\quad M_{\\rm RAM}\\le16\\ \\text{GB}.\n$$\n\n| Candidate | FPS, Live | $M_{\\rm VRAM}$ | $\\overline{\\mathcal L}_1$ | $\\overline{\\mathcal L}_1/\\mathcal L_{\\rm ceil}$ | Decision |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| <span class=\"wan-kept\">MVP2 result</span><br>$544\\times960,\\ F_{\\rm win}=32$ | $13.44$ | $7.1$ GB | $0.426$ | $0.58$ | User's choice by the grids: quality matters more than $1.5$ FPS |\n| $544\\times960,\\ F_{\\rm win}=24$ | $15.03$ | $6.8$ GB | $0.426$ | $0.58$ | Fails at a scene change |\n| $512\\times928,\\ F_{\\rm win}=32$ | $15.05$ | $7.1$ GB | $0.484$ | $0.66$ | Fallback point at 15 FPS |\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{cfg}_{\\rm MVP2}&:\\quad H\\times W=544\\times960,\\quad h\\times w=17\\times30,\\\\[6pt]\n&\\underbrace{M_H=\\tfrac{22}{17},\\quad M_W=\\tfrac{40}{30}}_{\\text{PI by the grid ratio}},\\quad\n\\underbrace{(F_{\\rm chunk},F_{\\rm anch},F_{\\rm win})=(8,8,32)}_{\\text{MVP1 video memory}},\\\\[6pt]\n&\\underbrace{W_{\\rm DiT}=\\mathrm{W4A4},\\quad W_{\\rm T5}=\\mathrm{BF16},\\quad\\mathrm{KV}=\\mathrm{FP8}}_{\\text{precision}},\\quad\n\\underbrace{\\mathrm{Attn}_{\\rm self}=\\mathrm{Attn}_{\\rm cross}=\\mathrm{Sage\\,2}}_{\\text{attention kernels}},\\\\[6pt]\n&\\underbrace{\\mathrm{FPS}=13.44<15}_{\\text{constraint not met}},\\quad\\overline{\\mathcal L}_1=0.426.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constraint $\\mathrm{FPS}\\ge15$ is not met at the chosen point. The missing $11.6\\%$ we look for in the kernels; we do not change the resolution and the window.\n\n[Live config](configs/candidates/544x960-win32-live.json) · [Experiment config](configs/candidates/544x960-win32-experiment.json) · [512×928](configs/candidates/512x928-win32-live.json)\n\n[Final grids](D:/Experiments/DIT/outputs/speed-2026-11-12/grids/) · [Report 11–12.11](docs/report-2026-11-11-loss-704-608-for-fable.md) · [Version summary](Версии%20исследования%20EN.canvas)\n","x":0,"y":2800,"width":1780,"height":1250}
	],
	"edges":[]
}